                             BREAKDOWN PRODUCTS

                            THEORY OF OPERATION

Overview
--------

The current register to operation algorithim exhibits a high degree of 
complexity. This is due to the fact that a separate pass exists for register
allocation on intermediate trees, and this code must mirror the sometimes
complex algorithims required to produce optimum code for different situations,
especially for CISC operations on processors such as the I80386.

A Solution
----------

The idea of breakdown products is to produce a series of increasingly small
operations out of the present large ones. This is already done in the encoder.
For example, a series of store directs such as tistoint are constructed only
within the encoder to recognize that a store to a fixed address is a special
case of the istiint intermediate.

The new operators would be processor independent, but their use or non-use
would be processor dependent. Lets go through an example.

tistoint takes a single intermediate tree and stores it into a directly 
specified variable represented by its base type. If the intermediate is an
immediate, represented by tilimint, it is turned into one of several 
instructions that store an immediate to a variable, as represented by 
genstoimmr.

If we recognize that, and generate the special instruction:

tistoimm

Which takes no trees, but has a base that is the variable, and a base that is
the immediate to store. This then takes a slice of complexity out of the 
tistoint instruction. Another example would be:

tiincvar

Which would have only one base, which is a variable, then increment it in place.

For a CISC processor, this would generate a large series of very specific
operations. However, they would still be understandable in a processor 
independent way. The tiincvar instruction would have no meaning in, for example,
a RISC design that is strictly load/store based. So it would only be generated
for a CISC design that needs it.

The appearance of such a series of breakdown products would simplify the coding
of individual cases, at the cost of creating more of them. It would also 
eliminate the need to have the register pass constantly mirror the work of the
coding pass below.

The breakdown product has two other features that make it attractive. First,
since it is processor independent (in operation if not in the set of breakdown
products), it can be incorporated as a machine independent pass.

Second, it is incremental, meaning that more and more special instructions can
be generated to strip individual cases out of the code and reduce complexity.

Table driven operator coining
-----------------------------

The running of the breakdown products pass can be table driven, which would 
allow its use in several different processors. The table would simply give a 
list of breakdown intermediates that the processor backend will accept. Then,
the breakdown products pass will enable or disable the finding and generation
of the products based on that.

Flat list code generation
-------------------------

If we have the rule that code generation routines are not allowed to examine
leaves, which this spec allows for, then the genxxx routines can change from
tree traversal to reforming the intermediate as a list based on that same 
traversal. This is arranged as a linear list of intermediates, as now, but
without the tree structure.

The advantage of this is it enhances the chances for further optimization.
Jump to jump elimination can be run, and if the new register assignment 
algorithm is run, the flat code can be used to bridge temporary use to register
use by successively replacing the shortest gaps where a registerable temporary
is used by a push and pop. This allows the register allocation to be dramtically
simplified.

